# Deterministic digitized data: force LF in the working tree on every platform so the digitizer
# determinism gate (tests/test_digitizer.py) is byte-identical on Windows and Linux CI alike. The
# digitizer writes LF (scripts/digitize_yamashita_fig3a.py); this keeps checkout from rewriting it.
openmucf/data/yamashita_kino_lc_T.csv text eol=lf
# Source figure: treat as binary (no line-ending or text munging).
openmucf/data/yamashita_kino_fig3a.png binary
# Malformed-by-design format fixtures: their exact bytes ARE the test. One is deliberately CRLF and
# must stay CRLF; the others are deliberately LF and must stay LF, or the checkout itself would
# decide which error they raise. -text disables end-of-line translation in both directions.
tests/fixtures/g4dat_bad/*.g4dat -text
# The generated conformance corpus, for the same reason and one more: members of it carry a byte
# outside the format's set on purpose, and a checkout that rewrote one would change which error
# code that file raises -- silently.
tests/fixtures/g4dat_conformance/* -text
# The zero-rows fixture the validator's `validate_zero_rows` test reads: the oracle's line splitter
# takes `#END` literally, so a checkout that appended a CR would turn the fixture into a different
# oracle grammar failure than the one the test is about.
tests/fixtures/g4dat_zero_rows/* -text
# The shipped dataset: byte-exact artifacts, for three separate reasons. example.prov.json is the
# byte range #SOURCEDIGEST is computed over, so a CRLF checkout would change its digest; example.g4dat
# is LF-only by the format's own rule (E006); and both, plus the snippet, are regenerated and
# byte-diffed by `make audit`, which would fail on any platform whose checkout rewrote them.
data/g4/* -text
# A SEPARATE line, not covered by the one above: a gitattributes `*` does not cross `/`, so
# `data/g4/*` does not match `data/g4/d1/...`. Verified rather than argued from the manual --
# `git check-attr text -- data/g4/example.g4dat data/g4/d1/d1_capture.g4dat` reported `text: unset`
# for the first and `text: unspecified` for the second. The D1 build has the same three reasons as
# the example, and this checkout has `core.autocrlf=true`, so without this line a fresh clone would
# rewrite the digested Layer-2 bytes.
data/g4/d1/* -text
# The D3 build, for the same reasons: its CSV inputs are parsed by loaders that refuse CR, and its
# Layer-2 files are digested byte ranges, so a checkout must not rewrite them.
data/g4/d3/* -text
# The primitive numerical evidence carries digested bytes in a nested directory.
data/g4/d3/reference/** -text
# The copy of release v1.3.0's D3 comparison file, pinned by that file's git blob id: a checkout that rewrote its line
# endings would break the pin (data/g4/d3/* does not cross into history/).
data/g4/d3/history/* -text
# The Geant4 patches: their added-file hunks are the reader's LF bytes and a test compares them
# byte-for-byte against cpp/include and cpp/src, so a checkout must not rewrite their line endings.
cpp/patches/*.patch -text
# The Geant4 Software License beside the patches: a test compares it byte for byte with the copy
# under third_party/geant4/, so a checkout must not rewrite its line endings.
cpp/patches/LICENSE -text
cpp/transport/** -text
# The stopped-muon application: the driver records beside every chunk it runs the sha256 of the binary built
# from these exact bytes, so a checkout must not rewrite them.
cpp/stopped_muon/** -text
# The vendored upstream sources: each one's IDENTITY is its bytes. Each is pinned by its upstream
# git blob id, which is a hash of exactly those bytes, so a checkout that rewrote its line endings
# would break the pin and with it the entire parity chain -- and would do so silently.
# tests/test_g4parity.py asserts each file carries no CR, so deleting this line names its own cause.
third_party/geant4/** -text
data/g4/d2/* -text
# The stopped-muon benchmark's files: a test pins the pre-registration's sha256 and every reduced cell
# records the sha256 of its chunks' compact text, so a checkout must not rewrite their bytes.
data/g4/stopped_mu/* -text
# The generated benchmark document: its generator writes LF, so a CRLF checkout would show a regenerated copy
# as modified; it is checked out with LF on every platform.
BENCHMARK_STOPPED_MU.md text eol=lf
cpp/tools/harvest_d2.cc -text
cpp/tools/harvest_natural.cc -text
cpp/tools/harvest_decay.cc -text
# The recipe-run fixture of tests/test_g4d3_recipe.py: the collector reads its files as MuDirac printed them
# (LF) and refuses a malformed line, so a checkout must not rewrite their line endings.
tests/fixtures/d3_recipe/** -text
# The MuDirac patch: `patch -p1` applies it to the release archive's LF sources, so a checkout must not rewrite its
# line endings; its default-output inputs are MuDirac input files read as written.
third_party/mudirac/** -text
